In article <2dhqorINN339@vuokko.uta.fi>, f1toyl@uta.fi (Topi Ylinen) writes:
|> 
|> No, I haven't seen the sources. At least not the part where those memory saving
|> (?) options are. But even without seeing them, I have figured out that the
|> game seems to have a lot of quest data (for example) in the memory all the
|> time. Why should your rogue be interested who the heck Lord Surtur is and
|> whether you can (or do) genocide him or not. (Or what's his ac etc.)
|> Mind you, this was just an example. Most *rarely-used* features - LIKE 
|> self-polymorphing - could be stored in separate files and read only when
|> needed. I mean, why keep all those features in memory *all* the time?
|> Or are there some which are not kept in memory?

Well...most of the Quest code is the same (the stats for a single monster
don't take much memory, and the levels themselves are kept on disk until
needed).  Also, Nethack code (on memory limited machines) is overlaid so code
that's not being used will be swapped out to disk.  (The '386 version of NH
uses virtual memory instead of overlays, but it amounts to a more efficient
version of the same thing)  Levels that you're not on are written to files on
the disk (and not kept in memory).  I think that the message base and the
help information could be kept on disk (maybe it is, I haven't looked) instead
of in memory, but the game is large!  (Adding the ability for monsters to use
items and the tree like structure of the dungeon probably added the most code
to the game)

